STEP 2 — VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENT
  Claude ran these commands to create an isolated Python environment:

    python3 -m venv venv
    source venv/bin/activate
    pip install -r requirements.txt

  Why use a venv?
  A virtual environment keeps your project's dependencies separate from
  system Python. This prevents version conflicts between projects.

  Dependencies installed:
    flask>=3.0.0       — Web framework
    requests>=2.31.0   — HTTP requests to fetch METAR data
    metar>=1.10.0      — METAR string parser

STEP 3 — FETCHING METAR DATA
  Data is fetched live from NOAA (National Oceanic and Atmospheric
  Administration) using a public URL:

    https://tgftp.nws.noaa.gov/data/observations/metar/stations/KJFK.TXT

  The file returned has two lines:
    Line 1: Observation timestamp  (e.g. 2025/05/05 18:56)
    Line 2: Raw METAR string       (e.g. KJFK 050856Z 28015KT ...)

  BUG FOUND AND FIXED:
    The original code took lines[0] (the timestamp) instead of lines[1]
    (the actual METAR). Claude identified and fixed this.

STEP 4 — PARSING THE METAR STRING
  The Python 'metar' library parses the raw METAR string into fields.
  Claude wrote a parse_metar() function that extracts:

    - Temperature in °C and °F
    - Dewpoint in °C and °F
    - Wind speed in knots and mph
    - Wind direction in degrees
    - Wind gusts
    - Visibility in statute miles and metres
    - Sky condition layers (FEW, SCT, BKN, OVC + altitude)
    - Ceiling altitude in feet
    - Pressure in inHg and hPa
    - Present weather (rain, fog, snow, etc.)

  BUG FOUND AND FIXED:
    Sky layers were being converted with str(), which gave raw Python
    object strings like:
      ('FEW', <metar.Datatypes.distance object at 0x7fc...>, None)

    Claude fixed this with a proper format_sky_layer() function that
    reads the altitude value correctly and formats it as "FEW015".

    Also fixed: ceiling detection was parsing string representations
    instead of reading the tuple directly, so ceiling was always null.

STEP 5 — FLIGHT CATEGORY CALCULATION
  Claude added logic to calculate the FAA flight category from ceiling
  and visibility:

    LIFR  — Ceiling < 500 ft  OR  Visibility < 1 SM    (Red)
    IFR   — Ceiling 500–999 ft OR Visibility 1–3 SM    (Yellow)
    MVFR  — Ceiling 1000–3000 ft OR Visibility 3–5 SM  (Blue)
    VFR   — Ceiling > 3000 ft AND Visibility > 5 SM    (Green)

STEP 6 — FLASK ROUTES
  Claude set up these URL routes in app.py:

    GET /                    → Home page with search form
    GET /search?q=<query>    → Search by name, city, ICAO, or IATA
    GET /metar/<ICAO>        → METAR result page (HTML)
    GET /api/metar/<ICAO>    → METAR data as JSON
    GET /airports            → Full airport reference table
    GET /api/airports?q=...  → Live airport search (used by autocomplete)
    GET /download/airports.csv → Download the CSV file

STEP 7 — UI WITH BOOTSTRAP 5
  Claude built the frontend using Bootstrap 5 (via CDN) with:

    - Navbar with search bar on every page
    - Home page with live autocomplete dropdown (JavaScript fetch API)
    - Result page showing all weather fields as cards
    - Dark card displaying the raw METAR string with a Copy button
    - Flight category badge (colour-coded)
    - Sky cover legend and flight category legend
    - "Try Another Airport" button at the bottom
    - Responsive layout (works on mobile)

STEP 8 — AIRPORT CSV AND NAME SEARCH
  Claude created airports.csv with 140+ airports including:
    ICAO code, IATA code, Airport name, City, Country

  This enables:
    - Searching by airport name (e.g. "Heathrow")
    - Searching by city (e.g. "London", "Delhi", "Tokyo")
    - Searching by IATA code (e.g. "LHR", "JFK", "DEL")
    - METAR results showing full airport name and location
    - A downloadable CSV reference file

